  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Find the Slope of a Line Using Points on a Graph</title>
      <para>The slope of a line is the ratio of the change in \(y\) and the change in \(x\). You can use the slope formula, \(m= \frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\), to find the slope of the line through points \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2, y_2)\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="4 points on a coordinate grid, origin O.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_03_04_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>Find the slope of the line that goes through points</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>\(A\) and \(D\)</item>
        <item>\(A\) and \(B\)</item>
        <item>\(C\) and \(D\)</item>
      </list>
      <para>Here’s how to find the slope given two points.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>
          <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - First, name \(B(-3, -4)\) as point 1 and \(D(4, 3)\) as point 2.
      \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2, y_2)\)
      \((-3, -4)\) and \((4, 3)\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the slope formula.
      \(m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values: \(y_2 = -3\), \(y_1 = 3\), \(x_2 = -1\), \(x_1 = 2\).
      \(m= \frac{-3-3}{-1-2}\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.
      \(m= \frac{-6}{-3}\)
      \(m= 2\)</para>
       </item>
      </list>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>
          <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - First, name \(A(2, 3)\) as point 1 and \(B(4, -1)\) as point 2.
      \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2, y_2)\)
      \((2, 3)\) and \((4, -1)\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the slope formula.
      \(m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values: \(y_2 = -1\), \(y_1 = 3\), \(x_2 = 4\), \(x_1 = 2\).
      \(m= \frac{-1-3}{4-2}\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.
      \(m= \frac{-4}{2}\)
      \(m= -2\)</para>
       </item>
      </list>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>
          <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - First, name \(C(-2, 5)\) as point 1 and \(D(-1, -3)\) as point 2.
      \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2, y_2)\)
      \((-2, 5)\) and \((-1, -3)\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the slope formula.
      \(m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values: \(y_2 = -3\), \(y_1 = 5\), \(x_2 = -1\), \(x_1 = -2\).
      \(m= \frac{-3-5}{-1-(-2)}\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.
      \(m= \frac{-8}{1}\)
      \(m= -8\)</para>
       </item>
      </list>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Find the Slope of a Line Using Points on a Graph</title>
      <para>Find the slope of the line that goes through points \(B\) and \(D\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="The graph shows the x y-coordinate plane. The x- and y-axes each run from negative 6 to 6. The point (negative 4, 1) is plotted and labeled “A”. The point (negative 3, negative 4) is plotted and labeled “B”. The point (1, negative 3) is plotted and labeled “C”. The point (4, 3) is plotted and labeled “D”.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_03_04_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
   </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to find the slope given two points:</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> 
    First, name \(B(-3, -4)\) as point 1 and \(D(4, 3)\) as point 2.
    \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2, y_2)\)
    \((-3, -4)\) and \((4, 3)\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> 
    Use the slope formula.
    \(m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> 
    Substitute the values: \(y_2 = 3\), \(y_1 = -4\), \(x_2 = 4\), \(x_1 = -3\).
    \(m=\frac{3-(-4)}{4-(-3)}\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> 
    Simplify.
    \(m=\frac 77\)
    \(m= 1\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
        <exercise id="_5a3625fa-17a7-4de9-a521-ef7e1f940bdb" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the slope of the line that goes through points \(A\) and \(D\)?</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Graph of a line, origin O.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_03_04_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(-\frac14\)</para>
            <para>\(\frac14\)</para>
            <para>\(–3\)</para>
            <para>\(3\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Check yourself: Substitute the coordinates of points \(A(-4, 1)\) and \(D(4, 3)\) into the slope formula: \(m= \frac{3-1}{4-1}=\frac14\).</para>
            <para>Incorrect. First, identify the coordinates of points \(A(-4, 1)\) and \(D(4, 3)\). Be sure to substitute the \(y\)-values in the numerator and the \(x\)-values in the denominator of the slope formula.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <para>Watch the following videos to learn more about finding the slope of a line.</para>
